 behind million  moments for democracy is the activation  of civil society and to make people to  realize that every single one of us has  responsibility and we can do something  and  change our way of thinking and change  even the politics alright it was some  very lofty goals there this young man  says he wants to change check politics  but Jonathan what exactly does he mean  by that well they said that they've  offered the Prime Minister and ultimatum  either he gives up his conflicts of  interests he resigns or they will  continue to protest and up the ante now  I think they would be the first to admit  that regime change is not going to  happen just like that because barbash is  very very popular still his party is  polling at 30% by far the most popular  party in the country and if you go  outside of Prague in the regions where  he's wooed older voters with promises of  more money more money in their pension  you know the party is still very popular  tie to that the opposition is quite  fragmented quite ineffective so there's  nobody really to challenge him even his  coalition partners of Social Democrats  won't walk out on the coalition because  they know they would face an electoral  obscurity so it is still going to be  very difficult for the prime minister to  go anywhere I think protesting is one  thing but regime change is probably  quite another  GW correspondent Jonathan crane  reporting for us from Prague the Czech  capital we thank you very much
 

